93
00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:06.050
So the main Shroot Farm was shot at the Disney Ranch, which is also called the Golden Oak Ranch.

94
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.750
It is a place where you shoot things.

95
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:14.270
It's like an outdoor exterior shooting studio, basically.

96
00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:15.830
It's massive, though.

97
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:16.550
It's massive.

98
00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:17.990
It's acres and acres, right?

99
00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:18.590
It's like...

100
00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:18.930
Oh, yeah.

101
00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:19.570
It's massive.

102
00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.950
And so, so many movies that need this sort of rustic look have been filmed there.

103
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.410
Like, the history of Disney Ranch is really cool.

104
00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:28.470
Oh, yeah.

105
00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:34.290
It's located in Newhall, California, which is about 17 miles from the Dunder Mifflin Studios.

106
00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.510
I'm giving you that detail because several people asked how far away it was from our main studios,

107
00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.490
and I want them to feel satisfied.

108
00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:46.150
So there are several barns and farmhouses on this property.

109
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:54.210
They chose one of them to be the Shroot Farmhouse, and then they created a graveyard next to it.

110
00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.010
But you might be thinking, wait a second, I don't remember a graveyard.

111
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.710
That's because it's a deleted scene.

112
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.290
It's a deleted scene.

113
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.310
It's a really good deleted scene, you guys.

114
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.170
If you have the DVDs, go watch it.

115
00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.030
So here's what happens.

116
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:16.430
They're in the cemetery because Dwight wants to show Ryan how many shroots have actually been buried on shroot farMs.

117
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:22.650
And he has, like, his grandfather's tombstone, and he's like, my grandfather was a good man but did very bad things.

118
00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.790
So what is up with his grandfather?

119
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:30.930
His grandfather was, I don't know, maybe not a great guy.

120
00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:36.650
Then Dwight finds, like, an empty, it looks like a beer can, and he gets furious.

121
00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:39.770
And he starts looking around, and he sees this couple.

122
00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.950
The guy, like, basically has no clothes on.

123
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.510
He's, like, covering himself with his pants.

124
00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:48.190
And a girl who barely is clothed, and they start running through the field, and Dwight's yelling at them.

125
00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:56.690
And this was awesome because it's a total callback to Office Olympics where Dwight mentions that teenagers sometimes use the beet farm for sex.

126
00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.490
Oh, I'm so bummed this is not in the episode.

127
00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:00.970
It's a great scene.

128
00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:02.290
It's a really funny scene.

129
00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:02.510
Oh, my gosh.

130
00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:03.990
I love a callback.

131
00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:09.290
And this is calling back creepy grandfather and the teenagers on the farm.

132
00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:10.690
And you know what?

133
00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:16.750
When I watched it, all I could think about, since I filmed many scenes on shroot farms, was the field they ran through.

134
00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.170
A, it was probably super hot.

135
00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:21.110
And B, it was snaky.

136
00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:22.470
We'll get to that later.

137
00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:27.870
But all I could think of was these people with not a lot of clothes on having to run through that snaky field.

138
00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:28.610
I was like, oh.

139
00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:38.370
Yes, let's talk about those snakes, Angela, because Kent told me that the one requirement for shooting on the ranch is that every production has to hire a snake wrangler.

140
00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:38.970
Yeah.

141
00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.630
Because the property has a history of rattlesnakes on the property.

142
00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:44.030
Yeah.

143
00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:45.370
Oh, yeah.

144
00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:50.350
And Kent said the snake guy found a rattlesnake near that graveyard set.

145
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.070
A five-foot rattlesnake, by the way.

146
00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.590
Oh, I don't doubt it.

147
00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:55.150
I don't doubt it.

148
00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:56.890
That's as big as you, Angela.

149
00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:58.290
It's my height.

150
00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:01.590
I have two more facts about Disney Ranch.

151
00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:02.590
Let's hear it.

152
00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:06.910
So the other location that we shot on Disney Ranch was that barn.

153
00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:07.670
Mm-hmm.

154
00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:10.310
That old barn, we also used that.

155
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:18.830
And we used another house at the Disney Ranch for that scene at the bar at the end when Dwight and Ryan are at the bar.

156
00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:26.750
That house that they used for the bar was originally built for Pee Wee Herman's show back in the 1980s.

157
00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.110
And they redressed it and turned it into a bar.

158
00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:29.890
Isn't that amazing?

159
00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:30.970
That's crazy.

160
00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:31.730
I know.

161
00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.070
So there's a little fun fact.

162
00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:33.730
Yeah.

163
00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:42.250
And finally, a lot of people wanted to know if we did keep going back to the same location to shoot year after year.

164
00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:48.810
And yes, we went back to the Disney Ranch multiple times, including in season four, the money episode.

165
00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.030
That is when Pam catches Moe's in the outhouse.

166
00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:53.110
Yeah.

167
00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:54.250
And then we were there.

168
00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.390
Dwight and Angela have a scene in that barn years later.

169
00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.850
But then ultimately, we'll get to this.

170
00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:04.210
The final Shroop Farm scene of the whole series is in a whole new location.

171
00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.270
That was so beautiful.

172
00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.170
But we'll get there.

173
00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:07.950
Yeah.

174
00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:08.970
That was gorgeous.

175
00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:09.750
It looked like we were in Scotland.

176
00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:10.610
It was so beautiful.

177
00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:11.410
Yes.

178
00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.190
But we don't go there till the end.

179
00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.330
Till the end end.

180
00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.030
Okay, guys, we'll get there in three years.

181
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:17.250
We'll get there.

182
00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:23.630
Fast fact number two, the beet fields were not shot at Disney Ranch.

183
00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.090
That was a completely different location.

184
00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.910
Those scenes were shot at the Underwood Family Farm in Moorpark, California.

185
00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.750
It is a real working 200-acre farm.

186
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:40.230
They grow corn, carrots, kale, strawberries, tomatoes, and, of course, beets.

187
00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:44.410
So we filmed those in an actual beet field.

188
00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.210
Isn't that crazy?

189
00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.190
That is crazy.

190
00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:51.330
I have been to that farm with Isabel, my daughter, for a school field trip.

191
00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:55.670
And I went on a hayride through, like, all around.

192
00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:56.070
Oh, yeah.

193
00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:58.470
It's like a huge farm.

194
00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.390
And it's just, it's not that far away.

195
00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.790
All right, moving us along to fast fact number three.

196
00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:06.170
This was the first episode directed by Randall Einhorn.

197
00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:12.610
And you guys, so Randall was our cinematographer and our A-camera operator.

198
00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.810
You've heard us talk about him a lot.

199
00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:15.710
We loved him.

200
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:16.510
We loved him.

201
00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:17.870
He was just so fun.

202
00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.030
And this was a, this was like a big deal.

203
00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.090
Like, the cast was so excited to see Randall direct an episode.

204
00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:27.230
I got on the phone with Randall, Angela.

205
00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:27.730
You did.

206
00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:28.510
I love him.

207
00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.650
Yes, and we're going to have him on the show.

208
00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:36.910
He told me that this was his first directing job ever.

209
00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:43.750
He told me that before our show, he had only ever worked on documentary style projects.

210
00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:47.530
And remember, we said he was a camera operator on Survivor.

211
00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:48.230
Yeah.

212
00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.650
But he had done all this other documentary work as well.

213
00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:52.590
Yeah.

214
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:58.590
He used to film like, I think like riverboat, crazy river rafting stuff in Australia.

215
00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.450
Like, he is like a tough guy.

216
00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.830
And I think the documentary style, like we said, was perfect for our show.

217
00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.550
And now he gets to direct.

218
00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:07.730
Yeah.

219
00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:12.030
He said he couldn't believe he got the job as our cinematographer because he had never worked

220
00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.130
in scripted anything before.

221
00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.330
And now Greg was telling him to direct.

222
00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:18.650
And he was like, what?

223
00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.290
But he said that Greg was really sweet.

224
00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:27.490
Greg said, Randall, all the time you are finding jokes with your camera.

225
00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:30.610
And this is a natural extension of that.

226
00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.050
And so Randall was like, all right, I'll do it.

227
00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:39.130
And he said he was so touched because he felt like everyone in the cast and crew was rooting

228
00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.110
for him to succeed.

229
00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:45.870
He said he'll just never forget what that support felt like and what it meant to him.

230
00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:49.070
He said, especially BJ, who was the writer of the episode.

231
00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:54.690
He said the hardest part of directing this episode.

232
00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:58.750
Because I said to him, I'm like, Randall, this was a huge episode.

233
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:03.090
You have to introduce Pretzel Day, Shroot Farms, and Moe's.

234
00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:06.730
And then it all ends with this epic phone call between Jim and Pam.

235
00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:09.250
What was the hardest part of all that?

236
00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:12.850
And he said the hardest part was not holding the camera himself.

237
00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:14.250
Well, yeah.

238
00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:16.350
Yeah, that's so true.

239
00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:18.850
Because that was his background for years, you know?

240
00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:22.810
But I think it made him such a great director because he had that vision.

241
00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.510
He knew where he wanted the camera.

242
00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.690
He knew what he wanted the camera to see.

243
00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:27.870
I loved that.

244
00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:32.930
Well, he said he did take the camera a few times during this episode.

245
00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:34.690
And he even pointed them out to me.

246
00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:38.010
So I'll tell you those moments as we go along.

247
00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:39.050
Oh, fun.

248
00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:44.790
And he also told me that even today, when he's directing, because that's what he does now.

249
00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:47.210
He's now a director exclusively.

250
00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:54.570
